Haemochromatosis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Profound fatigue/arthralgia, Bronze skin hyperpigmentation, New-onset diabetes, Elevated transferrin saturation

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-028
Reason for Visit: Profound, debilitating exhaustion, severe joint pain, and new-onset confusion/lethargy preventing the patient from standing or completing basic self-care.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, adult offspring.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted an acute, severe decline in functional status characterized by profound lethargy and possible metabolic derangement in a patient with progressive generalized symptoms and new-onset suspected diabetes. Urgent out-of-hours evaluation was required to exclude an acute diabetic emergency (DKA/HHS), assess for heart failure or severe hepatic decompensation, and expedite definitive hospital admission prior to the next available routine appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My body feels like it weighs a thousand pounds, every joint in my hands is screaming, and I can barely keep my eyes open."
History of Present Illness: Patient reports a 12-month history of progressive profound generalized fatigue and lethargy, acutely worsening over the last 48 hours. Notes severe arthralgia predominantly affecting the second and third metacarpophalangeal joints of both hands. Family notes a progressive generalized slate-grey/bronze hyperpigmentation of the skin in sun-exposed areas over the past year. Patient reports increased thirst and urination over the past month, suggestive of new-onset 'bronze diabetes'. Reports right upper quadrant abdominal discomfort. Male patient notes recent loss of libido and erectile dysfunction.
Functional Status: Bedbound, acutely lethargic, unable to ambulate without two-person assist.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of falls and metabolic collapse; totally reliant on family for care.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 105/65 | HR: 98 | Temp: 36.5 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 97% | Wt: 82 Kg
General: Patient appears chronically unwell, deeply lethargic but rousable, with noticeable generalized skin hyperpigmentation.
Physical Exam: Skin examination reveals diffuse slate-grey/bronze discoloration, particularly on the face and arms. Joint examination demonstrates bony enlargement and tenderness at the 2nd and 3rd MCP joints bilaterally without acute erythema. Abdominal examination reveals a firm, palpable liver edge 3 cm below the costal margin (hepatomegaly) with mild right upper quadrant tenderness. Cardiovascular exam reveals regular rhythm but trace bilateral pedal oedema. Point-of-care blood glucose is markedly elevated at 22.4 mmol/L. The presentation directly aligns with profound fatigue/arthralgia, bronze skin hyperpigmentation, and new-onset diabetes; elevated transferrin saturation is anticipated pending urgent laboratory testing.
Environmental Observations: Patient sleeping in a dark room; family visibly distressed by the rapid functional decline.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Haemochromatosis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute metabolic decompensation/New-onset Diabetes)
Differentials:

Addison's Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of marked hyperglycemia and hepatomegaly, whereas primary adrenal insufficiency classically presents with profound hypoglycemia and weight loss.

Chronic Hepatitis C - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The highly specific combination of the 2nd/3rd MCP arthropathy, slate-grey hyperpigmentation, and sudden onset diabetes, which are classic for iron overload rather than viral hepatitis.

Osteoarthritis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The profound systemic involvement (hepatomegaly, diabetes, severe fatigue) and the specific, atypical joint distribution (MCPs rather than PIPs/DIPs or weight-bearing joints alone).
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute metabolic decompensation (severe hyperglycemia) superimposed on the classic, late-stage stigmata of undiagnosed severe iron overload (bronze diabetes, hepatomegaly, specific arthropathy). The acute functional decline and marked hyperglycemia necessitated an immediate after-hours home assessment to rule out a life-threatening diabetic ketoacidosis or hyperosmolar hyperglycemic state. The patient's inability to maintain hydration and the risk of underlying iron-induced cardiomyopathy or cirrhosis required immediate referral for hospital admission to stabilize the blood glucose, evaluate end-organ damage, and initiate urgent phlebotomy/chelation pathways that could not safely wait for outpatient workup.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered 1L normal saline IV stat on-site to address acute hyperosmolar dehydration. Arranged direct admission to the acute medical unit via non-emergency ambulance for management of severe hyperglycemia and definitive diagnostic workup (iron studies, HFE gene testing, echocardiogram).
Positive Behavioral Change: While waiting for transport, drink one glass of water every 30 minutes. The extremely high sugar levels in your blood are drawing fluid out of your cells and forcing it into your urine; drinking consistently will directly replace this lost fluid, diluting the sugar and preventing your blood pressure from dropping dangerously low before you reach the hospital.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission required.

